;;; Relations.mon --- Finite facts and simple relational queries

:author "Laluxx"
:version "1.0.0"
:keywords "how-to relation predicate query symbol map converse"

;;; Commentary:

 A relation is a set of pairs.  It records which values are related without
 requiring each value on the left to have only one value on the right.

 A restaurant order is naturally a map because each order number identifies
 one meal.  People's tastes are naturally a relation because Alice may like
 both pizza and sushi:

     'alice -> 'pizza
     'alice -> 'sushi

 Relation literals use braces and arrows.  Map literals use hash braces:

     { 'alice -> 'pizza }   ;; Relation
     #{'alice 'pizza}       ;; Map

 The type ={a -> b}= means =Relation a b=.  For symbolic facts the compact
 type ={'a -> 'b}= means =Relation Symbol Symbol=; the names =a= and =b=
 describe the two roles rather than particular symbols.

 A relation can be called like a predicate.  Supplying both endpoints asks a
 yes-or-no question:

     likes 'alice 'pizza

 Leaving one endpoint as a fresh name asks for all matching values:

     who likes 'pizza       ;; people who like pizza
     'alice likes what      ;; foods Alice likes

 In a =show= sentence, the readable subject-first form is supported directly:

     show who likes 'pizza

 These queries are finite and strict because a literal contains finitely many
 facts.  Ordering is presentation order, but duplicate facts are observed only
 once.  Open-ended or recursive logic programming will use a lazy search type
 rather than changing this finite Relation contract.

 A Map is the left-functional part of Relation: every left value has at most
 one right value.  =functional?= checks that property.  =converse= swaps the
 two roles, turning "person likes food" into "food is liked by person".

 Try:

     monad how_to/Relations.mon && ./Relations

;;; Code:

import Data.Relation

module Main


;;; Declare some facts

define likes :: {'person -> 'food}
{ 'alice -> 'pizza
  'bob   -> 'sushi
  'alice -> 'sushi
  'carol -> 'pizza }


;;; Ground calls are Boolean membership tests

assert likes 'alice 'pizza
assert likes 'bob 'sushi


;;; A fresh endpoint is a query variable

show who likes 'pizza
show 'alice likes what


;;; Relations are extensional sets of facts

show (relation-size likes)


;;; A one-to-many relation is not map-shaped

show (functional? likes)

define favourite :: {'person -> 'food}
{ 'alice -> 'pizza
  'bob   -> 'sushi }

show (functional? favourite)


;;; Converse swaps the direction of every fact

define liked-by :: {'food -> 'person}
  converse likes

show food liked-by 'alice

